ause we can use  basically the model as in silicon oracle  we can perform thousands of these syntax  experiments and we can really start  tearing apart all the rules of  how you know sequence motifs and  spacing constraints density combinations  all of this influences binding of  thousands of proteins and these proteins  of course regulate gene activity right  now this is sort of a way to understand  just natural syntax right we're not  looking at natural genetic variants yet  uh but we can perform the exact same  kind of mutation experiments with real  genetic variants so here's an example of  an important genetic variant uh some  individuals have a c at this position  and this is known to actually previously  known to actually affect binding of a  particular protein okay so we train a  neural network model on that protein  the model has never seen genetic  variation it's just strained on the  genome  and then the model we use the model to  predict the effects of the two versions  of that variant this is the c version it  predicts a kind of a interesting profile  when we switch it to the g version you  can see this massive  amplification of the binding signal  right so this is great it validates  um you know this this variant has been  known to be uh affecting binding of this  protein and  is also associated with autoimmune  disease  uh the model correctly predicts that  this variant has a pretty big impact on  binding of this protein but as i  mentioned before we can use a model as a  lens and use attribution methods to  infer how this variant is affecting  binding and it tells a really  interesting story when this c when the  when the sequence of the c at this  position  uh you can see that dna world is  basically inactive there is another dna  word right here to the left that drives  this  weak signal  as soon as you switch the c to a g it  creates this really strong dna word and  this dna word and this dna word they  cooperatively affect binding and they  massively amplify binding so we get a  very nice interpretation  of  what exactly might be happening and it  can allow us to design experiments to  test these hypotheses as i showed you  before  so if you apply this to various diseases  like um  like here's an example of alzheimer's  disease right  so this is what's called a genome by  dissociation study and each point here  is a genetic variant there are millions  of these and the y-axis shows the  association of that variant with  alzheimer's so you can see most of the  variants end up doing nothing but there  are these long towers right  this is called a manhattan plot because  it kind of looks like the skyline of  manhattan these  these these are these towers of variants  these are  loci that appear to have strong  statistical association with azimuth  disease right  so we can go into any of these loci and  start dissecting which variants in this  loci are actually functional  so i'll show you one such example here  i'm showing you these kinds of molecular  profiles in  almost 60 cell types from the human  brain  and they look beautiful they can really  capture these elements and their  activity here you can see an element  that is only active  in oligodendrocytes so there's very  certain specific activity  and what we can do is train models on  each of these  so we have a model for every cell type  we can take any genetic variant of  interest and push it through the model  and predict the delta effect of the two  versions of any variant right  and so here's an interesting locus  there are about 165 variants in this  locus the causal variant is hard to pick  up just from an association study due to  various correlation  properties amongst these variants  we can use our models to pinpoint  exactly one variant right here  amongst these 165  that happens to fall in this  very cell type specific element in  oligodendrocytes  uh this element happens to loop and  regulate this picam gene as shown by  this data  and then we